\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces DLinear architecture. The $120\times 11$ input window is split by a moving-average kernel ($k{=}25$, replication padding) into a trend $\mathbf  {T}$ and a seasonal residual $\mathbf  {S}$; each is mapped to the $24$-step horizon by its own linear layer, the two forecasts are summed, and a learned cross-channel projection $\mathbf  {W}_{\text  {proj}}\in \mathbb  {R}^{11\times 7}$ produces the final $24\times 7$ forecast. DLinear-S shares $(\mathbf  {W}_t,\mathbf  {W}_s)$ across channels; DLinear-I gives each channel its own pair.}}{31}{figure.caption.40}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dlinear-arch}{{5.1}{31}{DLinear architecture. The $120\times 11$ input window is split by a moving-average kernel ($k{=}25$, replication padding) into a trend $\mathbf {T}$ and a seasonal residual $\mathbf {S}$; each is mapped to the $24$-step horizon by its own linear layer, the two forecasts are summed, and a learned cross-channel projection $\mathbf {W}_{\text {proj}}\in \mathbb {R}^{11\times 7}$ produces the final $24\times 7$ forecast. DLinear-S shares $(\mathbf {W}_t,\mathbf {W}_s)$ across channels; DLinear-I gives each channel its own pair}{figure.caption.40}{}}
